<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolás de Piérola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolás de Piérola was a Peruvian politician who, as finance minister, negotiated the Dreyfus contract for guano in 1869 and proclaimed himself supreme chief during the War of the Pacific. In 1895 his coalition of Democrats and Civilistas overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-avelino-caceres">Andrés Avelino Cáceres</a>, and as president from 1895 to 1899 he reorganised public finances and the army, opening the stable period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristocratic-republic">Aristocratic Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-pierola.jpg" alt="Nicolás de Piérola" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicol%C3%A1s_de_Pi%C3%A9rola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Piérola was born on 5 January 1839 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, to an aristocratic but impoverished, ultra-Catholic family, and he was known as El Califa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a>. He studied theology at the Seminario de Santo Toribio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and later took a law degree. He married Jesusa de Iturbide, believed to be the illegitimate daughter of Agustín Jerónimo de Iturbide y Huarte, a son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As finance minister under President José Balta from 1868 to 1871 he won broad powers from Congress to negotiate the guano contract with the Dreyfus house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Under Pardo's administration he was impeached for misappropriating funds and acquitted once Congress backed him. On 6 May 1877 his followers seized the ironclad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> in a bid to overthrow President Mariano Ignacio Prado; after the inconclusive Battle of Pacocha against two British ships the vessel surrendered, and Piérola left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>He returned in 1879, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. When Prado left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to buy arms, Piérola seized power after heavy fighting and took full control on 23 December 1879. He censored the press, so that El Comercio did not appear for three years, and promoted officers for loyalty rather than experience. Expecting the Chilean attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> from the north, he marched troops to Ancón; it came from the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> lost the battles of San Juan and Miraflores, and Lima fell in January 1881. Chile never recognised his government, and he resigned on 28 November 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>He founded the Democratic Party in 1889 and, allied with the Civil Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, raised fighters who occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and ousted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-avelino-caceres">Andrés Avelino Cáceres</a>; over a thousand people were killed during his entry into the city. Unopposed in the election of 1895, he received 4150 of 4310 valid votes. His term is generally held to have opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristocratic-republic">Aristocratic Republic</a>. He introduced the Libra Peruana, a gold coin of the same value as the British sovereign, and sponsored Japanese immigration. He lost the race for mayor of Lima in 1900, withdrew from the presidential election of 1904 and died in Lima on 23 June 1913.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicolás de Piérola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-16.4,-71.54&amp;year=1887" aria-label="Arequipa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2602 2773 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicolás de Piérola"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3052 3043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3052 3043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-16.4,-71.54&amp;year=1887">What was here in 1887? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
